# Instructions

- Use lowercase underscore naming for scene files.
- Use lowercase underscore naming for node names and all resources.
- Prefer simple node names: `sprite`, `shape`, `area`/`job_area`, `animation`, `shadow`.
- Match node names to packed scene or component script names (e.g. `squash`, `shadow`, `mouse_events`).
- When editing a scene, check a few existing scene files for patterns.
- Signals are always connected in scene files rather than in code when it is possible.
- Do not put `uid` references in the scene files you create, only add path references and let Godot hook up the correct uid later. When you need Godot to regenerate or assign scene/resource `uid` references, use the `godot-cli` skill instead of asking the user to open the editor manually.

# Composition

Here is how I compose the game: world > objects > components

The world contains various autoloads and system objects in the root, then there's usually one main container with Y-sorting enabled which contains the game objects.

# Game objects

- Objects are made out of components.
- Objects usually have one root script which defines the object for the others and exposes public state and functions.
- Components are usually private and build the behaviour of the object. Objects usually don't interact with or know about internal components of other objects.
- I prefer constructing game objects out of components declaratively, like making them from reusable well-defined lego pieces, instead of coding all behaviour in the root script.
- Components encapsulate reusable behaviours. Object-specific logic should go into its root script.
- Some object scenes are not valid in total isolation. They may expect runtime data, autoload state, or supporting nodes from the full world scene. When smoke-checking such scenes, expected context/setup errors do not automatically mean the object is broken.

Usually a complete game object has these parts:

- Sprites
- Shadow
- Sound effect players
- Animation player
- Squash component
- Mouse interaction components
- Flag (command) interaction components

# Juice checklist for objects

- Reacts to hover and clicks by the mouse
- Reacts to interactions with other objects
- Squash component, squishy reactions
- Animations with scaling of the sprite for emphasis, random start for animations
- White outline effect on hover
- Sound effects for all interactions
- Random timers for everything
